    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `summarize_ticket` in `app/summary.py` and all 3 tests pass. One cleanup is left for you: I couldn't delete the temporary test file `tests/test_tmp_summary.py` because removing it needed approval. You can delete it or keep it as a regression test.\n\n```python\nmessages = [SystemMessage(content=SUMMARY_PROMPT), HumanMessage(content=ticket_text(ticket))]\nreply = chat.invoke(messages, stop=[\"\\n\\n\"])\nreturn str(reply.text).strip()\n```\n\n- **One request:** it makes a single `invoke` call with the system prompt and the rendered ticket, and passes `stop=[\"\\n\\n\"]` so generation stops at the first blank line.\n- **Reading the reply:** I used `reply.text` rather than `str(reply.content)`, which is what `translate.py` does. Some chat models (Anthropic's, for example) return a list of content blocks instead of a string. With `str(content)` you would get the list printed as Python code rather than the summary. `.text` pulls out just the text in both cases.\n- **API check:** Context7 couldn't be used because its monthly quota is used up. Instead I checked `invoke`'s `stop` parameter and the `.text` property against the installed langchain-core 1.6.5 source.\n\n**Test:** the temporary test uses LangChain's built-in fake chat model. It returns a reply made of content blocks with extra whitespace, and checks four things:\n- exactly one call is made\n- the stop sequence is `[\"\\n\\n\"]`\n- the messages are the system prompt followed by the rendered ticket\n- the result is the trimmed plain string\n\nThe same bug is probably in `translate_replies`: it uses `str(message.content)` too. I didn't change it because it's outside this task."
